Guard the tag wipe in psa_aead_finish() against a failed buffer copy

psa_aead_finish() copies its two output buffers locally (LOCAL_OUTPUT_ALLOC)
before doing anything else. When either allocation fails it jumps to `exit:`
with the local `tag` pointer still NULL, and there calls

    psa_wipe_tag_output_buffer(tag, status, tag_size, *tag_length);

which returns early only for a zero `tag_size`, and otherwise memsets
`tag_size` bytes at NULL. The result is a segfault instead of
PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY.

zucrypt's allocation-failure sweep found it on its first run over AES-GCM
(Stage 14a): failing the ninth allocation of a GCM encryption killed R with
"address (nil), cause 'memory not mapped'", reproduced on macOS with an
interposer whose backtrace put the failed calloc in
psa_crypto_local_output_alloc() under psa_aead_finish().

psa_mac_sign_finish() in the same file already guards its identical call with
`if (mac != NULL)`. This patch adds the same guard here and nothing else.
With it, that allocation failure returns PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY, which
the adapter reports as ZUC_ERR_MEMORY.

Local, until upstream fixes it. Re-check on every release bump whether
psa_aead_finish() still needs it.

--- a/lib/psa_crypto.c
+++ b/lib/psa_crypto.c
@@ -5606,7 +5606,12 @@
      * Even if the operation succeeds, make sure we clear the rest of the
      * buffer to prevent potential leakage of anything previously placed in
      * the same buffer.*/
-    psa_wipe_tag_output_buffer(tag, status, tag_size, *tag_length);
+    /* zucrypt: guarded as psa_mac_sign_finish() guards its own call. If a
+     * LOCAL_OUTPUT_ALLOC above fails, `tag` is still NULL here, and the
+     * unguarded wipe wrote tag_size bytes through it (0003). */
+    if (tag != NULL) {
+        psa_wipe_tag_output_buffer(tag, status, tag_size, *tag_length);
+    }
 
     psa_aead_abort(operation);
 
